{
  "id": "COMPOUND:15491 - PROTEIN:4299",
  "verbalization": "Derrisin is implicated in the targeting of Sodium/Potassium Transporting ATPase subunits through a series of molecular interactions involving Cytochrome c4 and other compounds. The evidence suggests that Derrisin acts as a target for multiple compounds, which in turn target the Sodium/Potassium Transporting ATPase subunits. These compounds, such as (4aS,6aR,6aS,6bR,8aR,10S,12aS,14bS)-2,2,6a,6b,9,9,12a-heptamethyl-10-octanoyloxy-13-oxo-3,4,5,6,6a,7,8,8a,10,11,12,14b-dodecahydro-1H-picene-4a-carboxylic acid and 6-O-Acetyldihydrohelenalin, are known to interact with the ATPase subunits. This implies that Derrisin may function as a mediator or modulator in the signaling pathways involving these subunits, potentially influencing their activity or function. The consistent targeting of the ATPase subunits by these compounds, coupled with the direct interaction of Derrisin with Cytochrome c4, suggests a possible role for Derrisin in the regulation of the ATPase activity, though the exact mechanism remains to be elucidated.",
